A||||29||GT Bastard||An observation on higher education...||||8||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||02-15-2001||01:31 PM||||Maybe I'm completely wrong, BUT... It seems like people aren't going to school to do what they're interested in.  The trend seems to be moving towards getting educated to make more money.  Like all the college and tech school ads on the radio.  The focus is almost completely on making money(namely the computer industry.)  Most of the people I work with are going to school for networking or something computer related, but nobody really seems interested in talking about computers.  It's all:  "That Windows2000 server class is a pain in the ass.  I can't wait till I graduate." etc... etc...  I wonder if they even realize what's happening to them.  I'm not saying everyone who pursues this type of career feels this way, but it seems to be the majority.  They've basically sold-out for money and social acceptance.  <P>uhh... yeah.... That's about all folks.||||reg||8
Z||000001||Black88GT||02-15-2001||01:42 PM||||I agree.  Its like that movie "Office Space"  I know its a comedy but in a way its a view on how people who hate their job but make good money live.  I don't see the point in doing a job you hate just because your getting paid well.  Most people that do that end up trying to change their careers when its too late when they realize money doesn't buy happiness.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||sqoach||02-15-2001||01:52 PM||||that's how it is over here too. my parents and other people tell me i need to go to college so i can get a good job and make good money. but i hated college. that's why i quit going after the first semester. it's just boring and it wastes my time. i enrolled in four classes, dropped two of them, and at the end of the semester only passed one. and it happened to be a computer class. ok go ahead and laugh at me now.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig2.JPG"> <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||02-15-2001||02:42 PM||||I think that money should be a high priority (not the highest obviously, but up there). I would like to do something tech related, but I'm not going to, because i need more money..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||02-15-2001||02:53 PM||||Going to school so that you can get a good job when you get out...  is that what you call "hire education"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">?<P>Anyway, I'm thankful to have a pretty good job, doing something I (generally) enjoy doing.  And I'm glad I went to school back when I was still pretty sharp, and interested in lots of things.  Seldom worried about being able to make "big money" after I got out - mainly worried about money for next semester.  Looking back, I don't think I'd want to change much  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.      ||||reg||12
Z||000005||Cooter||02-15-2001||02:57 PM||||I make decent money, live in a decent house and drive a Fiero. My job has its good and bad points. I hate the paperwork, but I love the kids. So, I will never be rich from teaching, but when a kid says, "daddy. . .  I mean Mr. Gaines, I need some help"<BR>it makes it a little more worthwhile.<P>------------------<BR>Just because you can't do it doesn't mean it can't be done.||||reg||14
Z||000006||p4n1c||02-15-2001||03:04 PM||||I'mn interested in computers, went to college for networking, slacked off on all my courses cuz I was bored, I actually don't have my diploma because of calculus.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I think most of the time it is people interested in the stuff they are going to school for, it's just not challenging and they get bored or pissed off that it's boring.<P>I'm in my job for the money for now.  Because I have none.  I need to make money and then go onto something I like.  Not saying I don't mind where I am, but it's not computer related(AT&T Canada as a communications tech).  Money and opening options first, fun a little later down the road.  <P>Hell I'm only 20, I have my whole life ahead of me, might as well make the big bucks now, get AT&T on my resume and then find something I enjoy 2-3 years down the road. <P>Open up options to yourself with the big boys, the experience is well worth it, they pay for training and you have countless resources at your fingertips for learning stuff you are interested in, then move onto that.<P>That's my .03 CDN||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tigger||02-15-2001||03:15 PM||||Lots of people always drop out of my degree program at calculus.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Euler's/Bernoulli's equations, laplace transforms, quadratic equations, eigenvectors, and eigenvalues oh my.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 02-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||p4n1c||02-15-2001||03:19 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tigger:<BR><B>Lots of people always drop out of my degree program at calculus.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I want to know what calculus has to do with computer networking though.  I asked and their answer was "Because that's the only way we can be acredited with universities."  That's the stupidest excuse.  My dad is a nuclear operator and he doesn't even use the crap.  <P>I know some of it(like imaginary numbers) are useful for hardcore electronics, but that's about all I've seen. <P>I said screw it and found a job without a diploma.  I still gotta go back and get the piece of paper, but it can wait for a bit.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>"Each passing day, every passing face seems like such a blur" <BR>- The Offspring(Million Miles Away)<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 02-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||N_Man||02-15-2001||04:15 PM||||well I think that youre only half right GTB.<P>Being half way through my degree right now, I would say most people i know that were trying to do somthing just for money do not make it far at university.<BR>I have seen more poeople then i can count drop out of the computer science program, or even out of university all together.  All because they started with big dreams and when they realized that it was to hard or boring then they reevaluated their position and have gone elsewhere.<BR>BUT at the same time I see a minority of people slugging their way through programs even though they arent satisified with what they are doing because of the big money promise... oh and 90% of these people are coming from the engenineering department.<P>About classes being boring because you know it all, that is the biggest load of BS that I have ever heard... If you know what the class is all about dont go, and write the test at the end of the course.  If the class truly bores you its probably because you and the material arent compadable.<P>And as for myself money is definitly a concern but by no means dictates what im taking at school.  I am taking physical geography, and it doesnt promise to make me rich, but I should be making good money at a job i will like.||||reg||2
Z||000010||Tigger||02-15-2001||04:39 PM||||Calculus isn't easy, but you just have to dredge thru it.  Really, the longer you wait to take it, the worse it will be.  You use a ton of algebra and trig, and the longer you take it after those classes the more you'll struggle.  <P>My choice to go into engineering was simple, I like cars, and that's why I'm going to college.  Just wish I had more time and money to work on my own right now. (ugh, sucks)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||p4n1c||02-15-2001||05:00 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by N_Man:<BR><B>About classes being boring because you know it all, that is the biggest load of BS that I have ever heard... If you know what the class is all about dont go, and write the test at the end of the course.  If the class truly bores you its probably because you and the material arent compadable.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well it can't be BS, because I did know all the material, didn't goto class and still aced the tests and got through the computer courses just fine.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I was bored and I did know close to all of the material presented in the classes with the exception of Novell and some NT stuff.  I do however agree that school and I are incompatible, I can learn fine on my own, but when I'm in a classroom I get bored or I just can't think about the stuff being presented.<P>And no, I don't have an ego problem, I don't make people think I know it all, and I know I don't know it all.  Actually I make them think the opposite, that I know nothing.  Steve doesn't think I know anything which is why I think he tries to explain stuff to me.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I'm not into money for social acceptance, I could care a less what people think about me, I want money so I can do the stuff that I want to do.<P>Anyway, I don't want to start a flame war or debate, that's just my opinion and my views.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>"Each passing day, every passing face seems like such a blur" <BR>- The Offspring(Million Miles Away)<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 02-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||mrfixit58||02-15-2001||05:23 PM||||Well, I can relate to a lot of what was said here. I too, slugged it out through college. It wasn't easy. My degree is Electrical Engineering (yes, I hated calculas). I can't say that my job is always fun but, more good than bad.<P>After high school and four years in the Air Force, I had decided to attend the local tech school to augment my electronics I learned in the AF. Then, my uncle (who is an engineer) invited me and my wife to tour their facility. Then, he opened up this black book (literally) and showed me the difference between a tech-ie's salary and an engineer and that was all it took. Six years later, I are one.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Class of 87!<P>Someone once said, "You can do something you really like for a living or, get an education and make some real money". <P>My advice is, keep plugging away at your degree. Even if you decide to do something different, you'll have something the "other" guy doesn't have when competing for the same job.<P>Good Luck,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000013||DaRkLoRD||02-15-2001||05:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by p4n1c:<BR><B>Steve doesn't think I know anything which is why I think he tries to explain stuff to me.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>want me to explain why that is?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>LOL.. j/k<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||10
Z||000014||Richard Parnell||02-15-2001||05:44 PM||||I can relate to working in a field which I really have no interest in other than making money.  I worked and still do (for now) in the telecommunications industry for 20 some years.  I was unhappy and bored.  It affected my entire life and marriage.  I felt resentful and in return was not real pleasant to be around.  I should have sat my wife down years ago and explained to her that I needed to do something else with my life and that we probably would be taking in less money for a while.  But I didn't I was so wrapped up in making the max dollars .  We never hurt for anything except happiness.  Older, wiser, sadder.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Old Lar||02-15-2001||08:01 PM||||Calculus was a pain.  I guess I lucked out because I work at something that I enjoy.  Now if I could get rid of some of the PIA coworkers I'm stuck with.  It is the people you work with that make that make the work fun, but it seems that there is always one person that make life miserable at work.<P>Growing up in the 60's, you were expected to go to collage, but if you look around, carpenders, plumbers, mechanics or painters etc, if they are good, always have ready work.  Computers are not new, I even took a course or two in 1967 (IBM cards) no easy programming back then, confusing and I got out of that.  Computers do run my life now but it is using programs that the (computer geeks)programmers write.  I don't understand a thing on how the programs are written, only if they work.<P>As many have stated, the course work gets boring quite easily, I like the practical course I've taken like auto body repair, welding and fun things, even practical chemistry courses(my line of work).  But those theoretical courses  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000016||Hank is Here||02-15-2001||09:36 PM||||I feel it is important to do something that you like.  On that note where do you go to be a professional slacker?  j/k   The truth be said I don't like working for others on a time schedule, I would rather be self employeed, but I need a job to make money before I can start a business.  <BR>I like transportation, planes trains, automobiles and all.  Since no college has a transportation degree I am taking business logistics.  Logistics is the movement of products, information, and cash in a business. I am happy because I can still work with transportation but also work in other stratigic areas as well.  The other good thing that I like, hopefuuly, if I play my cards right there will be a lot of travel domestic and abroad.  Logisitcs looks at process flows a lot, so it seems to be more project based rather than a 9 to 5 job.  I like the area I am in, don't get me wrong I don't love it,  but I am happy with logistics for now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||Phil||02-15-2001||11:20 PM||||Retirement is better.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Yes I had calculus and loads of chemistry too. Lar- I did the computer thing with punch cards also FORTRAN 4<P><p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 02-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||terryk||02-16-2001||12:36 AM||||A four year degree is the equivalent of two years experience in an industry, but you're treated like you discovered fire. <P>If there is any doubt, get a degree. You might hate it while you're there, but you will never wish you hadn't.<P>Hollerith cards. I thought I was the only one that had to deal with them!||||reg||1
Z||000019||frontal lobe||02-16-2001||12:45 AM||||I had had LOTS of school.  A lot of it didn't seem worthwhile at the time, but later turned out to be worthwhile.  A lot DIDN'T turn out to be worthwhile, even in retrospect.<P>It doesn't even prepare you for the intended job a lot to times, but it often DOES open the door for you and give you an opportunity to show what you can do.<P>So I think if there is ANY chance that you might go to a job that values a degree, it's usually worth taking the chance and getting it.  You pay for it up front by suffering through boring classes, but if it gets you a good job, the payoff goes on for years.||||reg||1
Z||000020||151 in. of love||02-16-2001||12:58 AM||||I am in school for automotive technology.<BR>I love it, and the money will be great when I'm done. That's not the point, the point is that you should- <BR>"do what you love, and love what you do." <P>A lot of the time things fall into place when you are really delving into your work and happy. People are attracted to happiness and if you attract people the chips tend to fall better. <BR>My minor is Marketing so that when the world switches to the barter system I can still swim. I LOVE SCHOOL. the people, the learning, the challenge, the WOMEN!<P>"You can steal everything I own and take my job, but you can never rob me of my education" <BR> Money is secondary, at best. The wise ones know this already, and live fulfilling lives.||||reg||1
Z||000021||rogergarrison||02-16-2001||01:51 AM||||Ill prob open a can of worms but....to me a college is great if the career you want to pursue has the need for further schooling like law, medicine. For trades, I think a technical school is better way to go. What I have seen in years past is college trained people being what they call 'overgualified'. Most companies are tightening belts and they have different pay scales between college and non college job applicants. That means its in the company policies to pay college grads more money for similar jobs. What happens is they will hire a non grad to do a job simply because hes cheaper. Others just hire the grad because hes a grad, not for his ability. There are lots of people without who can do any of the jobs with on job training. In my case I chose to save the huge amount of money college cost and learn a job I liked by doing. Now I have owned my own business for 15 years, go wherever and whenever I want, fly planes, play with cars, work maybe 25 or 30 hours @ week and make more than enough money. My total debt is prob less than $2000-$3000 and thats all credit card. I own my house/garage, 2 nice vehicles including my Ferriero, and my Cessna 172. I didnt take a 'make a zillion' course, just hard work. Funny thing is I get college people coming to me to look for a job. I think that kind of ironic. Im not really knocking going to college if thats what you want...but I can say for firsthand...you can go places without it too. You asked for opinions.<P>thanks<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||kinboyatuwo||02-16-2001||12:06 PM||||WAIT A SECOND ON THE GENERALIZATIONS<P>Sorry to yell but i hear it too<P>I am a 22 yr old who left high school early and started and finished my tool and die apprenticeship (ok still cant spell).  Decided because i did not like the career and job style and it was not rewarding even though it paid realy well, to leave go back to HIGH SCHOOL and then go in debt to go to university to try to be a high school physical education teacher.  Now in my sceond year I am following my dream and not looking or concerned with the pay cut i will take<P>So there are some people that are out there for the love of a job or dream.<P>------------------<BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."||||reg||8
Z||000023||fierospeeder||02-16-2001||01:50 PM||||Im studying for a BA in Math and computer science.  The only way i can get a BA in computer science is to take the BA in math also, they are combined in the Liberal Arts and Science College.  The ba for math is BS.  <P>I have to take tons of math classes.  Yes I am also trying to struggle through calculus.  I have taken calculus for a few times, but its due to me ditching class too much that i had to retake it.  I loved math in highschool, and geometry and algebra was so fun but calculus doesn't seem that fun.  Phsycis was the only class i got an A in college.<P>I dont think people should get into computers if they have a hard time putting a 3.5" disk in. If people are saying programming etc.. sucks then imagine working 8 or more hours programming everyday.  Its the best paying job out there plus a few more like being CEO.  <P>If car mechanic was a 100 an hour job, would there be everyone getting a BA in auto mechanics?  Eventhough, they dont know that clockwise is to tighten a nut.<P>School does suck, i noticed that all im really doing is to buy a book from school, which is available anyplace, and going to class to learn from that book.  I have taught myself a lot of things, reading on my own, and i learned a lot about auto mechanics just reading on my spare time while not doing homework.<P>Tests are my worst problem in school.  Studying for the wrong things is one problem.  Finding out theres more difficult questions on the test then what I thought.  Sometimes the professors or the departments have strict rules.  Sometimes a course has 2 or 3 tests and if you bomb a test, I'm pretty much screwed if I do bad on the 2nd.  Or if the "curve" isnt used when 90% of the classes grade on a test was B.  <P>I have about 1 to 2 years left.  Im not taking a lot of classes and just going through school very slowly.  Its probably a 5 year plan.  I know i can get a degree right now in computer science.  But i was looking for jobs because my part time job sucks in money and i seen that a BA is a must in programming plus experience.  I think i can fullfill the experience by working in the summer at some place that hires college students to do small jobs on the computers part time.  I cant wait till i get that sweet paying job so i can spend millions of fieros plus a few other cars.  <P>A fiero with a northstar engines and 2 superchargers.  Six speed manual trans, leather seats.  When the engine gets hot, some nitrous for it.||||reg||1
Z||000024||p4n1c||02-16-2001||03:18 PM||||Programming is great, the math needed for 3D programming, 2D programming and just programming in general is great and easy to do, because you don't have to solve just figure out the variation of an equation to use, it's good to know how to solve it though to plug in values and get an answer to double check results.  <P>Math classes in college/university suck.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I do program in C and math is quite important.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>"Each passing day, every passing face seems like such a blur" <BR>- The Offspring(Million Miles Away)<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 02-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||fropuff||02-17-2001||07:32 PM||||151 in of love:  I was wondering what field of automotive technology you are going into.  I am still trying to figure out what I want to do.  I wanted to be an anesthesiologist, but then I thought of the long hours, years of college and med school, and paying back the crapload of student loans.  I was also thinking of an architect, but they don't make crap until later in the job.  I want to do something with cars.  I like bodywork, so that might be a past time hobby.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Phil||02-17-2001||09:26 PM||||Most employers like to see a degree not so much for the book learnin' ya got , but rather to see if you have the ability to take on a project and "slug it out" or just say "this is too much of a hassle" and quit.||||reg||1
Z||000027||Standard||02-18-2001||02:24 AM||||I figured out about 2 or 3 years ago that I don't care how much money I make, as long as I'm doing something I like.  I could be MCSE and novell certified right now, my dad has all the books and programs I would need so I could ace the tests no problem, but the thought of sitting inside staring at a computer screen all day just isn't something I want to do.  So, other than slacking around alot  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">, I've decided to go to Tech school for engine rebuilding/machining etc.  And if that doesn't pan out I can always fall back on the computer stuff.. I have been messing with computers for quite a few years longer than I have with Fiero's.<P>It was kinda hard earlier in the year, with all my friends in college and me without a job, I felt like I had no direction and no meaning.  Of course, I don't think I really did, but that's beside the point  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  <BR>But after listening to my friends *****  about tuition and homework and the fact that they weren't really sure what they were going to do made me feel a bit better.  After a few nights of thinking and meditating with the beer bong when I visit my friends, I realized that I just have to stop worrying about it and do what I want to do.  I just try to live my life as worry free as possible.  It may seem like I'm drifting through to some people, but happiness is worth way more to me than money.  My dream is to someday open up a Fiero shop up here, or to work in the high-performance car division of a major car company, or even just a speed shop.  Or if I ever get really fit I wouldn't mind racing mountain bikes for a living...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000028||danny boy||02-18-2001||02:54 AM||||I was never big on School. I tried twice to get my H.S diploma- first senior year I just didnt get the job done,second year I missed a full month due to illness and couldn't catch up, so I bit the bullet and got my GED.<BR> I apprenticed in HVAC for a while, then decided it was time to make something of myself. I decided to go to school for CADD, but after 5 weeks of sitting behind a computer 8hrs a day I knew it wasn't for me. Then I went on a run with my Uncle in his truck.Hmmm, So let me get this straight, I can go to School 4 days a week for 4 weeks, get a CDL and be guaranteed a job before I even graduate? Where do I sign up? And that is how I became a Truck driver. I made good money, got to see the country,and REALLY enjoyed my job. then I had to quit.I was told either get a local job or find another girl to marry. Now my paychecks arent half what they were,the work is twice as dangerous(I haul gasoline), I definately don't have that pride thing goin on, but I am home everynight and can work on my cars when I want.I still am glad I didnt tuff it out and follow through with the CADD operator thing.||||reg||1
Z||000029||151 in. of love||02-20-2001||07:51 PM||||Fropuff-<P>In our classes we emphasize a lot on managerial skills and presentations. Many of the classes are hands on. Thats where you start at, then you move on to technical writing and the major computer programs. None of the people who get out go wrench. The majority is Technical Assistance and Managerial applications. <P>There are so many different opportunities in this field it is unbelievable. If you want to be corporate there are more than enough opportunities. If you want Product Validation and testing it is limitless. There are not enough people for these huge companies that know the whole aspect of automobiles. <P>They come to you, they call and email and set up interviews. These are not only the big 3, these are all car companies dying for people with technical skills and professional outlook.<P>There are only 5 schools that offer this degree. <P>Personally, I am more interested in the business aspect. My minor is in Marketing, but I never went to school to get a job. I went to school to learn how to start a job.<P>If I was really interested in becomming monetarily wealthy, I never would have went to College. <BR>||||reg||1
